WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
Hi it's me Michelle from Lab Muffin 
Beauty Science, your resident skincare

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.620
science nerd. Today I'm going to be
talking about dry and dehydrated skin

00:00:08.639 --> 00:00:13.500
and the different ways to treat them. You
might have noticed that your skin gets

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:19.199
flaky in cold and dry weather. It might
make you feel prickly or itchy or like

00:00:19.199 --> 00:00:25.140
your skin's stretched tight across your
face. How do you fix it? Well, that depends

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:34.920
on whether you have dry or dehydrated
skin. Dry and dehydrated skin are similar

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.520
in lots of ways.
Both are worse in cold and dry weather,

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:43.770
can cause flaky skin, and can be treated
with moisturizers and other skin care

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:50.010
products. But here's the big difference:
dry skin lacks oil and dehydrated skin

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.940
lacks water.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:01:00.080
Dry skin is a skin type which means it's
built into your skin and isn't going to

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.820
change too much. It means that your skin
doesn't produce enough oil and it's the

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.640
opposite of oily skin. Oil is important
in your skin to keep it flexible and to

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.520
keep it waterproof. It stops water from
getting in and getting out of your skin.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:21.470
It also stops other chemicals from
getting in and out. How much oil your

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:26.060
skin produces mostly depends on your
genes but it can also be affected by

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:31.520
diet, stress, and the weather. Skin gets
drier when you age and oil production

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.450
can be reduced by some medications like
hormonal birth control,

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:41.869
accutane or isotretinoin, and
spironolactone. If your skin is producing

00:01:41.869 --> 00:01:45.530
the right amount of oil, in the
Goldilocks zone of not too little and

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:51.470
not too much, it's called normal skin. If
different parts of your skin fall into

00:01:51.470 --> 00:02:01.789
different categories,
it's called combination skin. Dehydration

00:02:01.789 --> 00:02:05.630
is commonly referred to as a skin
condition since it can happen to anyone

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:10.660
at any time and your skin's hydration
levels can change suddenly, very quickly.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:17.060
Any skin type - dry, oily, normal or
combination - can lose too much water and

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:21.560
become dehydrated. Hydration is important
for your skin.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:26.300
Having the right level of water in the
right places keeps your skin flexible,

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:31.400
plump and smooth. It also makes sure that
the normal biological processes inside

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:36.650
your skin are working properly.
For example, desquamation, the shedding of

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:41.870
dead skin cells, doesn't work properly
without proper hydration. Usually

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:46.010
dehydrated skin happens when your skin
barrier is weakened so it can't

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:50.660
effectively slow down water from
evaporating. This water evaporation is

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:56.050
called transepidermal water loss or TEWL.

00:02:56.050 --> 00:03:01.520
Dehydration can be caused by lots of
things: cold dry weather, overwashing,

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.610
overexfoliating and wind or sun exposure. In
general dehydrated skin isn't caused by

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:11.180
not drinking enough water,
unless you're drinking a dangerously low

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:21.710
amount. Now how can you work out if your
skin's dry or dehydrated? Firstly let's

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:26.720
talk about skin type. To work out if you're
oily or dry, see which of these two

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:31.520
descriptions fits your skin better. Oily
skin usually has large pores and

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:37.310
blackheads especially in the t-zone: your
forehead, chin and nose area. It's more prone

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:42.440
to acne and breakouts. If you have oily
skin you might have visible shiny

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:47.180
patches of oil on your skin or see
greasy spots on your pillowcase. If you

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:51.680
touch your face you might get some oil
on your fingers. You probably don't use

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.980
too much moisturizer and you might
notice that your makeup slides around

00:03:54.980 --> 00:04:00.800
during the day. On the other hand if you
have dry skin you probably have really

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.900
tiny pores and no visible shiny oil
patches on your skin. But your skin will

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:10.840
be more prone to cracking, rough patches,
fine lines and visible flakes.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.740
Moisturizer probably sinks into your
skin very easily but makeup settles into

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:20.299
fine lines and creases on your face. If
you can't tell from these descriptions

00:04:20.299 --> 00:04:25.130
you can try testing using some blotting
paper. A few hours after washing your

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:29.690
face, press the paper onto your nose. If
it sticks to your skin or shows a greasy

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:32.030
spot
you're probably oily and if it doesn't

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:39.650
you're probably dry. Hydration level is
pretty straightforward.

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:45.410
Unlike dryness there's no happy medium: 
you want your skin to be as hydrated as

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:51.770
possible. Dehydrated skin feels tight and
stiff instead of soft and plump. Pinching

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:55.940
it might cause stiff wrinkles. It can feel like
you have a tough film sitting on your

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:59.750
skin.
In general if your skin is dry then it's

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:03.620
likely to also be dehydrated as well,
unless you look after it really

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:09.050
carefully since oil helps your skin
retain water. If your skin is oily and

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:14.150
still feels tight then you almost
definitely have dehydrated skin. Oily and

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:18.290
dehydrated skin feels like a bit of a
paradox: it's like it's oily and dry at

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:26.630
the same time, like there's oil sitting on
top of parched leathery skin. Now that

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:30.410
you know whether your skin is dry and
lacks oil or is dehydrated and lacks

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:40.030
water, or both, let's talk about how to
fix it. Let's start with dry skin. Dry

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:45.860
skin needs oil so obviously we need to
put oil back into your skin. There are

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:50.620
two categories of oil ingredients in
skincare: emollients and occlusives.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:54.950
Sometimes reading about these terms can
get a bit confusing since they're used

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:59.840
in different ways even by dermatologists
and cosmetic formulators so keep that in

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:05.810
mind. These are the definitions I'm using.
Emollient ingredients sink into the

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:10.940
skin making it more flexible and
smoothing it out. Occlusive ingredients

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:15.650
partially block water from evaporating.
They won't form a perfect film that

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:21.650
stops everything from evaporating, but
they will help. Emollient ingredients

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:26.240
include most natural plant oils like
jojoba oil, natural skin components like

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:32.540
cholesterol and ceramides, and stearate
and oleate esters. Occlusive ingredients

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:38.690
include petrolatum or petroleum jelly,
paraffin, mineral oil, dimethicone and

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:43.760
lanolin. There are also some less
occlusive ingredients that still work as

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:47.229
occlusives
like shea butter and waxes like beeswax.

00:06:47.229 --> 00:06:52.100
Most occlusives are emollient as well and
emollients are usually also slightly

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:58.500
occlusive so the two categories aren't
mutually exclusive.

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:03.280
If your skin is dry you'll want to look
for moisturizers that have occlusives

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:08.139
and emollients high on the ingredients
list. In general products with a heavier

00:07:08.139 --> 00:07:12.810
texture will be richer in these oil
ingredients and suitable for dry skin.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:18.220
Some moisturizers that are really
popular for people with dry skin are The

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:24.340
Ordinary Natural Moisturizing Factors and HA,
Cerave Night Cream, Clinique

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:31.770
Moisture Surge, Kiehl's Ultra Facial Cream
and First Aid Beauty Ultra Repair Cream.

00:07:33.389 --> 00:07:40.060
You can also try using facial oils like
pure plant oils or oil blends. They're

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:43.870
really great on their own if you don't
have dehydrated skin or if you have a

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:48.669
bunch of sensitivities and have to avoid
a lot of ingredients. They also work

00:07:48.669 --> 00:07:54.789
really well in multi-step routines.
Popular oils include argan oil, rosehip

00:07:54.789 --> 00:08:01.720
oil, avocado oil, squalene and squalene.
Coconut oil is also popular but it can

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:05.639
make some people break out really badly.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:11.950
It's not just moisturizers that can help
out dry skin - you might also want to try

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:16.180
looking into cleansers that contain oils
as well to stop your cleansing routine

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:20.590
from washing off too much oil from your
skin. Non-foaming cleansers and cleansing

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:25.450
oils are good for cleaning skin without
stripping it. French pharmacy brands like

00:08:25.450 --> 00:08:30.490
Bioderma La Roche-Posay and Avene have
some great non-foaming cleansers that

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:36.760
people with dry skin love. Nivea and QV
have some really great products too. You

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:40.419
can get cleansing oils from lots of
brands like Simple, Shu Uemura,

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:47.319
DHC, Dermalogica, and Hylamide.

00:08:47.319 --> 00:08:51.829
Micellar waters are good too if you want
a less oily product, but I'd recommend

00:08:51.829 --> 00:08:59.930
rinsing them off afterwards like you
would with a regular cleanser. Dehydrated

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:05.329
skin needs water but unfortunately just
splashing more water on your face isn't

00:09:05.329 --> 00:09:10.579
actually helpful. If your skin gets
waterlogged it gets leakier so it's

00:09:10.579 --> 00:09:15.860
worse at holding on to its water. Instead
what you're looking for is firstly

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:20.990
humectants. Humectants are ingredients
that bind to water and stay on your skin,

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:26.959
sort of like little sponges that sit on
your skin and keep it wet. Examples of

00:09:26.959 --> 00:09:33.410
humectant ingredients are glycerin,
sorbitol, glycols, PCA, hyaluronic acid,

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:41.420
amino acids and urea. Alpha hydroxy acids
like glycolic acid and lactic acid also

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:49.999
work as humectants. For my dehydrated
skin I always look for moisturizers with

00:09:49.999 --> 00:09:54.800
humectants high on the ingredients list.
They usually come in lighter textures

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:59.209
that are really great for oily skin. Some
of my favorite moisturizers with

00:09:59.209 --> 00:10:03.230
humectants for dehydrated skin are
Neutrogena Hydro Boost Water Gel and

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:12.980
La Roche-Posay lotion, Toners containing
humectants are also fantastic since you

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:17.180
can use them as a weightless layer in
your skincare routine. My favorites are

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:22.550
Klairs Supple Preparation Toner and Jurlique
 Activating Water Essence. Hada

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:27.620
Labo Lotion is also really popular. You
can also use slightly heavier humectant

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:32.509
serums like Indeed Hydraluron. Your 
skin also naturally contains minerals

00:10:32.509 --> 00:10:37.970
called the natural moisturizing factor
that acts as humectants. Thermal waters

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:41.689
are a really good way of adding these
minerals back into your skin after they

00:10:41.689 --> 00:10:45.220
get rinsed out after washing.

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:51.560
I also look for cleansers containing
humectants for my dehydrated skin since

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:55.910
they help replace the humectants that
cleansing strips away. I particularly

00:10:55.910 --> 00:11:00.800
like Nivea Soothing Cleansing Mousse, QV
cleansers and Neutrogena Hydro Boost

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:09.110
cleansers. I also only wash my face with
cleanser in the evening and I just use

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:14.000
plain water in the morning, so I keep my
skin barrier as intact as possible, since

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:17.930
cleansing is probably the most damaging
thing you're forced to do to your skin

00:11:17.930 --> 00:11:23.180
on a regular basis. This cuts down on
your skin's exposure to surfactants that

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:31.460
make it leakier. Occlusives can also be
really good for dehydrated skin because

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:35.120
once you've got some humectants on
your skin you can seal the water in with

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:39.230
an occlusive product like Vaseline,
sleeping masks or other heavy

00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:42.890
moisturizers that have lots of
occlusives. Some of these occlusive

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:47.300
ingredients I've mentioned before:
petrolatum, paraffin, mineral oil,

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:53.810
dimethicone, lanolin, shea butter and
beeswax. If your skin is dehydrated and

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:57.350
oily you'll probably want to avoid
occlusive ingredients during the day

00:11:57.350 --> 00:12:02.300
since they tend to be heavy and greasy,
but I found that for my oily and

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:06.620
dehydrated skin if I use occlusives on
top of my usual skin care products when

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:11.120
I go to bed at night it's fantastic for
giving me an overnight facial sort of

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:15.110
effect. And I don't really care if I'm
loaded up with moisturizer and look

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:19.780
really really shiny in the safety of my
own room.

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:28.350
There are a bunch of other things that
you can do to help your dry or

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:33.480
dehydrated skin suffer less. Protecting
your skin from sun, heat and wind is

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:37.980
important to keep it happy. Air
conditioning can also dry out your skin

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:42.720
so you might want to look into a
humidifier if it's unavoidable. Avoid

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:47.759
long hot showers. Harsh cleansers and
soaps now quickly strip your skin of its

00:12:47.759 --> 00:12:53.160
natural oils and humectants. Products
that are high in SD alcohol, also called

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:58.049
ethanol, ethyl alcohol, or denatured
alcohol, can also speed up water

00:12:58.049 --> 00:13:03.989
evaporation. While ethanol binds to water
like a humectant, it evaporates instead

00:13:03.989 --> 00:13:07.829
of staying on the skin so it can
actually evaporate and take water along

00:13:07.829 --> 00:13:12.959
with it. It also disturbs your skin
structure a little which means it can

00:13:12.959 --> 00:13:18.029
increase water evaporation and it can
wipe away oils as well. But there aren't

00:13:18.029 --> 00:13:22.019
any other permanent effects so if
there's a product that you love that has

00:13:22.019 --> 00:13:26.369
high alcohol, just make sure you use lots
of moisturizing ingredients along with

00:13:26.369 --> 00:13:31.379
it. Using harsh products too frequently
can also make your dry or dehydrated

00:13:31.379 --> 00:13:36.480
skin unhappy. Rough physical exfoliants
and drying active ingredients like

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:42.569
retinoids and benzoyl peroxide can make
dry and dehydrated skin worse. That's a

00:13:42.569 --> 00:13:47.160
guide to dealing with dry and dehydrated
winter skin. Of course your skin can be

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:52.079
dry or dehydrated year-round and these
tips will apply as well. Thanks for

00:13:52.079 --> 00:13:55.799
watching my video! If you liked it please
subscribe to my channel. You can also

00:13:55.799 --> 00:13:59.699
follow me on Instagram and Facebook if
you want more science-based beauty tips,

00:13:59.699 --> 00:14:03.360
and of course I've also got my blog on
which I've got tons and tons of beauty

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:18.220
science. See you next time for more nerdy
talk about beauty! :)

